    "id": "prop:unmarked-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In the standard special case \\(Y(a)=1\\) almost surely for every \\(a\\), one has \\(q_x=f_x\\), \\(G_x=F_x\\), and \\(\\mu(a)=1\\); the probability-channel part of \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}marked\\text{-}cocycle\\text{-}id}\\), with location normalized only by \\(E_P[U_2]=0\\), reduces to Theorem 1 of Capitao-Miniconi, Gassiat, and Lehéricy.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(Y(\\cdot)\\)",
      "\\(q_x\\)",
      "\\(f_x\\)",
      "\\(G_x\\)",
      "\\(F_x\\)",
      "\\(\\mu\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:factorization",
      "thm:marked-cocycle-id",
      "thm:adrf-id"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction checks that the marked construction preserves the established unmarked model.",
    "gap": "This is a compatibility result rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "It allows implementations to reuse the existing unmarked analytic-sieve path when the mark carries no information.",
    "proof_tex": "Suppose \\(Y(a)=1\\) almost surely for every \\(a\\in\\mathcal A\\).  Consistency gives \\(Y=1\\) almost surely, and therefore \\(m_x(a)=1\\) for the conditional-regression version on the support of \\(f_x\\).  Hence\n\\[\n q_x(a)=m_x(a)f_x(a)=f_x(a).\n\\]\nThe two definitions of the observable channels now give, for all \\(s,t\\),\n\\[\n G_x(s,t)=E[Ye^{i(sS_1+tS_2)}\\mid X=x]\n =E[e^{i(sS_1+tS_2)}\\mid X=x]=F_x(s,t),\n\\]\nand the causal definition gives \\(\\mu(a)=E[Y(a)]=1\\).\n\nThe cited \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}capitao\\text{-}theorem\\text{-}one}\\) applies to the probability channel: compact support of \\(A\\) implies its condition (H2), and \\(\\mathrm{ass{:}error\\text{-}coordinate\\text{-}independence}\\) is its condition (H1).  That theorem identifies the latent and error laws up to their common translation.  In the present scalar model, \\(E_P[U_2]=0\\) fixes this translation, exactly as the derivative calculation in \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}marked\\text{-}cocycle\\text{-}id}\\) shows.  Thus, after deleting the now redundant marked channel \\(G_x=F_x\\), the probability-channel argument is the stated normalized special case of their Theorem 1.",
